[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Primary Key Generation Strategies in JPA"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Selecting the right primary key generation strategy is crucial for application performance, database portability, and concurrency. JPA provides the @GeneratedValue annotation, allowing developers to choose among four primary strategies: IDENTITY, SEQUENCE, TABLE, and AUTO."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The IDENTITY Strategy"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The IDENTITY strategy relies on an auto-incrementing column in the database. The database generates the primary key value upon insertion. While simple, it prevents batch inserts in some JPA implementations because the ID must be retrieved immediately after the insert statement executes."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
private Long id;"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. The SEQUENCE Strategy"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The SEQUENCE strategy uses a database sequence object to generate unique values. This is the preferred strategy for databases that support sequences natively (such as PostgreSQL and Oracle). It allows for optimizations like allocation size, enabling high-performance batch insertions."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "user_seq")
@SequenceGenerator(name = "user_seq", sequenceName = "app_user_sequence", allocationSize = 50)
private Long id;"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. The TABLE Strategy"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The TABLE strategy uses a dedicated database table to persist and manage primary key values. Although it offers maximum database portability, it is generally discouraged for high-traffic applications due to locking overhead and performance bottlenecks."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.TABLE, generator = "id_gen")
@TableGenerator(name = "id_gen", table = "id_generator", pkColumnName = "gen_name", valueColumnName = "gen_val", allocationSize = 1)
private Long id;"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"4. The AUTO Strategy"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The AUTO strategy delegates the choice of generation strategy to the JPA provider (Hibernate). The provider selects the strategy based on the underlying database capability. In modern Hibernate versions, AUTO typically resolves to SEQUENCE if the database supports it."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.AUTO)
private Long id;"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices Summary"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use SEQUENCE for PostgreSQL, Oracle, and databases supporting native sequences to allow batching optimizations."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use IDENTITY for MySQL or SQL Server when legacy auto-increment columns are strictly required."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid TABLE unless cross-database portability without native sequence support is a strict architectural constraint."}]
